 That such a $k$ exists, at an explicit bound depending only on the degree and
 not on the weight, is a theorem of Ikenmeyer and Kandasamy
 \cite[Lem.~5.2]{IK} for power sums $x_1^{D}+\dots+x_m^{D}$ in the regime
-(number of variables) $m\ge D$ (degree): unconditionally for $D$ even, and for
+(number of variables) $m\ge D\ge3$ (degree): unconditionally for $D$ even, and for
 $D$ odd under the side condition $\binom{2(D-1)}{D-1}\ge 2(m-1)$.  Their
 argument is algebraic and makes no optimality claim.  Everything in this paper
 concerns the exact minimal index and the boundary geometry that computes it.
